{"blocks":[{"content":"Challenge: Strict determinism implies evidence is a pure function of the problem input. If so, displacement of completed evidence is only a performance concern (recompute) unless the process ingested non-reproducible oracle inputs. The entire reclamation safety problem may reduce to whether the workspace boundary includes hidden non-determinism.","handle":"SCR_001","why_keep_this":"Reframes the safety guarantee as either a strong consensus problem or a weak caching problem, depending on the scope of determinism; tests the premise that evidence is intrinsically irreplaceable."},{"content":"Speculative contradiction: Treat the workspace not as mutable state but as a Merkle-DAG of pending side effects. 'Reclamation' becomes a merge of unmaterialized effects into the trunk. Evidence is never displaced because it is a content-addressed node, not a file path; reclamation only reassigns unmerged leaves. This eliminates the notion of 'silent displacement' structurally rather than protocol-wise.","handle":"SCR_002","why_keep_this":"Pushes the design space from crash-recovery protocols to data-structure invariants; asks whether the problem assumes an outdated mutable-workspace model."},{"content":"If the deterministic workspace nevertheless interacts with wall-clock time or physical randomness (e.g., Monte Carlo seeds from thermal noise), does the crash irrevocably destroy information that makes evidence impossible to reproduce, thereby upgrading 'silent displacement' from a liveness bug to an irreversible epistemic loss?","handle":"SCR_003","unfinished":"Unresolved question","why_keep_this":"Related advisory scratch: NEW_001"}],"guides":[],"links":[{"from":"SCR_001","handle":"SCR_004","relation_hint":"NEW_002 dissolves the tension raised in NEW_001 by removing mutability; NEW_001 exposes the hidden-oracle risk that makes NEW_002's Merkle-DAG necessary.","to":"SCR_002"}],"state_seq":500,"warning":"Scratch material is non-authoritative.\nIt is advisory, 